The '''Los Bronces mine''' is a large [[mining|copper mine]] located in central [[Chile]], on the border of the [[Santiago Metropolitan Region]] and the [[Valparaíso Region]]. The processing plant is located at 3,300 m above sea level, with mining operations extending up to 4200 m above sea level. Los Bronces represents one of the largest copper reserves in [[Chile]] and in the world having estimated reserves of 3.13 billion tonnes of ore grading 0.32% copper.<ref name="Iacobeni">{{cite web|url=http://www.angloamerican.com/~/media/Files/A/Anglo-American-Plc/media/fact_book/copper_2012|title=Copper mines|year=2011|publisher=angloamerican.com|accessdate=2013-07-06|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130202103617/http://www.angloamerican.com/~/media/Files/A/Anglo-American-Plc/media/fact_book/copper_2012|archive-date=2013-02-02|url-status=dead}}</ref>

The [[ore concentrate]] produced in the main contained as of 2020 about 4% [[chalcopyrite]], 32% [[chalcocite]] and 45% [[pyrite]].<ref>{{Cite journal |title=Processing of Complex Materials in the Copper Industry: Challenges and Opportunities Ahead |journal=[[JOM (journal)|JOM]] |last=Alvear Flores |first=Gerardo |url=https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11837-020-04255-9.pdf |volume=72 |issue=10 |last2=Risopatrón |first2=Carlos |last3=Pease |first3=Joe |year=2020 |doi=10.1007/s11837-020-04255-9}}</ref>

Following the [[Chilean nationalization of copper]] in the early 1970s stateowned company [[ENAMI (Chile)|ENAMI]] came to own the mine which was by then known as Disputada de Las Condes. In late 1977 ENAMI sold the mine to [[Exxon]] for 93 million US$.<ref name=ciperbronce>{{Cite news |title=Las desconocidas operaciones de Anglo que bloquean el acceso de Codelco a la propiedad de Los Bronces |last=Ramos |first=Marcela |date=2011-12-02 |url=https://www.ciperchile.cl/2011/12/02/las-desconocidas-operaciones-de-anglo-que-bloquean-el-acceso-de-codelco-a-la-propiedad-de-los-bronces/ |access-date=2025-06-10 |work=[[Ciper]] |last2=Figueroa |first2=Juan Pablo |language=es}}</ref> Exxon later sold the mine to [[Anglo American plc]] for 1300 million US$ in 2002.<ref name=ciperbronce/><ref>{{Cite news |title=Disputada: De Exxon a Angloamerican |date=2011-10-13 |url=https://www.df.cl/empresas/disputada-de-exxon-a-angloamerican |access-date=2025-12-28 |work=[[Diario Financiero]] |language=es}}</ref> 

In Los Bronces mining folklore tells of man known as "El Futre" (lit. "The Snob").<ref name=Sonia2015>{{Cite book|title=Mitos de Chile: Enciclopedia de seres, apariciones y encantos|last=Montecino Aguirre|first=Sonia|publisher=[[Catalonia (publisher)|Catalonia]] |year=2015|isbn=978-956-324-375-8 |page=297|chapter=Futre, El |language=es}}</ref> El Futre is said to be a formally dressed man who appear in drifts murdering or scaring workers in order to steal their salary.<ref name=Sonia2015/>  

The mine holds [[The Copper Mark]] environmental certificate.<ref name=bnamericas>{{Cite web |title=The role of the Copper Mark in responsible mining |url=https://www.bnamericas.com/en/interviews/the-role-of-the-copper-mark-in-responsible-mining |date=2022-09-09 |access-date=2025-08-21 |website=Bnamericas}}</ref> It lies in an area of [[rock glacier]]s.<ref>{{Cite journal |title=Minería y glaciares rocosos: impactos ambientales, antecedentes políticos y legales, y perspectivas futuras |journal=[[Revista de Geografía Norte Grande]] |last=Brenning |first=Alexander |volume=47 |pages=143-158|url=https://www.scielo.cl/pdf/rgeong/n47/art08.pdf |last2=Azócar |first2=Guillermo|year=2010|language=es}}</ref>

== Geology ==
{{expand section|date=December 2025}}
Mineralization at Los Bronces is thought to be due to its position at the intersection of two large [[fault (geology)|fault systems]]. This favoured the rise of magma and the subsequent circulation of mineral-rich fluids.<ref name=Piqueretal1019>{{cite journal |last1=Piquer Romo |first1=José Meulen |last2=Yáñez |first2=Gonzálo |last3=Rivera |first3=Orlando |last4=Cooke |first4=David |date=2019 |title=Long-lived crustal damage zones associated with fault intersections in the high Andes of Central Chile |url=http://www.andeangeology.cl/index.php/revista1/article/view/V46n2-3106/pdf |journal=[[Andean Geology]] |volume=46 |issue=2 |pages=223–239 |doi=10.5027/andgeoV46n2-3108 |access-date=June 9, 2019|doi-access=free }}</ref>

